<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gebel Adda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jebel Adda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a> was a fortified hilltop town on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, near the modern border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. Occupied from the Meroitic period into Ottoman times, it is generally identified with Daw, the late medieval seat of the Nubian kingdom of Dotawo. Excavated in the 1960s before the Aswan High Dam was filled, it now lies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gebel-adda.jpg" alt="Gebel Adda" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mileham, Geoffrey S; Randall-MacIver, David · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gebel_Adda.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
